<!--
  TEMPLATE — copy this to a new file named like your-slug-here.md inside
  content/public-thinking/, fill in the fields, delete this comment block,
  and write the body below the second `---`.

  This file is named TEMPLATE.md.example on purpose (not .md) so Eleventy's
  content/public-thinking/*.md glob never picks it up and accidentally
  publishes it as a real page.
-->
---
title: "Piece title, written the way a human would say it out loud"
slug: "url-safe-slug-no-spaces"
date: 2026-08-01
format: "Research Finding"          # One of: "Research Finding" | "Executive One-Pager" | "Case Study" | "Field Note"
filterType: "research"              # Matches the filter buttons on /public-thinking/: research | onepager | case | field
confidence: "Observation"           # MUST be one of: "Observation" | "Emerging Pattern" | "Research Finding"
                                     # These are the ONLY public-facing confidence labels — see Communitygeeks OS
                                     # → Public Source Pack → "Public Thinking — Confidence Vocabulary Map".
                                     # Never use internal labels (Low/Medium/High/Validated, or Weak/Emerging/Strong)
                                     # here. The Eleventy build will throw an error if this field has anything else.
summary: "One or two sentences. Used as the page <meta description> and the Article schema's description."
topics:                              # Optional but recommended. 2-4 short topic/entity labels this piece is
                                      # actually about (not the format — that's `filterType` above). Feeds the
                                      # Article schema's `keywords` field, giving search engines and AI systems a
                                      # structured signal beyond parsing prose. Derive these from what the piece
                                      # itself is actually about — don't invent a taxonomy that isn't there yet.
  - "Example Topic One"
  - "Example Topic Two"
accentColor: petrol                 # Optional. One of: petrol | brass | ink. Controls the one substantial
                                     # color field this piece uses on the Public Thinking index (if it becomes
                                     # the featured record) and on its own article masthead — same field drives
                                     # both, so a piece keeps one identity across surfaces. Omit to default to
                                     # petrol. Pick deliberately per piece; don't assign colors on rotation.
authors:                            # One or more names, byline order. Each name is looked up in
                                     # src/_data/people.js for a linked profile, photo, and bio in the article's
                                     # author box and structured data — a name with no entry there still renders
                                     # as a plain byline. Only add someone here once they've explicitly consented
                                     # to being named publicly (same rule the old `contributor` field carried —
                                     # see Public Source Pack → "Not Yet Ready for Publication"), and only add
                                     # a real people.js entry once they're actually a bylined public author.
  - "Carmelito Bauer"
heroImage: null                     # Optional. Path like "/assets/images/public-thinking/your-image.jpg" if you add one.
                                     # Also becomes the Article schema's `image` — falls back to the sitewide
                                     # og-image when not set.
dateModified: null                  # Optional. Set only when a published piece gets a substantive revision —
                                     # falls back to `date` otherwise.
pdf: null                           # Optional. Path to a downloadable PDF under public/ if this piece has one,
                                     # e.g. "/downloads/moat-or-funnel.pdf".
related:                            # Optional. List of {slug, title} pairs for other Public Thinking pieces.
  - slug: "some-other-piece-slug"
    title: "That other piece's title"
geometry:                           # Optional. This piece's own background geometry (see geo-plate.njk) —
                                     # positioned behind/beside the OPENING of the body as an environmental
                                     # field, not a standalone graphic the reader is meant to stop and read.
                                     # It's absolutely positioned and width-clamped (never width:100% of the
                                     # article column — that's what makes it a quiet field rather than a
                                     # dominant object), so it adds zero document height: hiding it changes
                                     # nothing about the body's own flow or spacing. Omit entirely for a piece
                                     # with no geometry — a normal, allowed choice, not a missing feature. Do
                                     # not assign a fixed shape-to-meaning mapping (e.g. "circle always means
                                     # X") — compose each piece's field on its own editorial merits, favoring
                                     # asymmetry, cropping, and unequal scale over anything that reads like an
                                     # infographic (e.g. two same-size overlapping circles reads as a Venn
                                     # diagram — avoid that specific shape unless deliberately intended).
                                     # shapes: type (circle | semicircle | rect | triangle | line), the type's own
                                     # geometry fields (cx/cy/r for circle, x/y/w/h for rect, points for triangle,
                                     # x1/y1/x2/y2 for line, d for semicircle), color (petrol | brass | ink only),
                                     # and opacity (keep it very low — this is a watermark, not an illustration).
                                     # viewBox just needs internally-consistent proportions for your shapes; the
                                     # CSS host clamps rendered width to ~220–360px regardless, so there's no
                                     # square-viewBox trap here the way there was for the old boxed layout.
  viewBox: "0 0 320 420"
  shapes:
    - { type: circle, cx: 300, cy: 50, r: 150, color: ink, opacity: 0.06 }
    - { type: rect, x: 30, y: 190, w: 230, h: 170, color: petrol, opacity: 0.09 }
---

Write the body here as normal Markdown. Headings, bold, links, lists all work.
This is what actually renders on the piece's page — check it against the
Public Source Pack's Governance Rule before publishing: internal OS research
language is evidence for what you write here, not text to paste in directly.

If the piece makes more than one distinct sub-claim, give each its own `##`
heading. Not a rigid template to fill in — just makes each claim independently
findable/linkable, and easier for a reader (human or otherwise) to cite one
point without needing the whole piece. Skip it for a short, single-point piece.
